<p>Why do you get out of bed in the morning?</p>
<p>Are you excited and tantalized by the prospects for the day ahead? Looking forward to one or more activities you're going to do? Luxuriating in the thoughts of the human interactions today will bring? Because you have a lot on? Because you're being paid to?</p>
<p>Or (f), none of the above?</p>
<p>For as long as I can remember, whenever anybody has bewailed getting old and having a "landmark" birthday, I feel the obligation to ask them to "consider the alternative".  The alternative to getting old is, of course, dying first.  It's the only way to avoid it.  Now, if when you reach 60 or 70 or whatever, your reaction is "hasn't the past decade been great! I can't wait to roll into the next one", I'm not going to say this.  But if you say, "aren't I getting old?" you are, whether you have formed the conscious thought or not, saying there is something bad about being alive <i>in certain circumstances</i>, those circumstances being correlated in your mind with your age.</p>
<p>At the same time, you often hear people say some version of "well, they never gave up" or "I don't know how she carries on", apparently seriously.  But what does that actually mean?  What does it mean "to give up on life" and, depending on what you think it means, can you actually tell if somebody has given up or not?  Fundamentally, we are talking about choices, and there isn't really a choice: you have to keep putting one foot in front of the other.</p>
<p>Why?  And should we have to?</p>
<p>So let's say that, right now, you have one or more good reasons to get out of bed in the morning.  Let's consider some alternative scenarios, either that you might find yourself in or that other people do find themselves in and consider whether you would still (want to) get out of bed in the morning.</p>
<p>Let's start with the obvious: imagine you literally can't.  If you were bedridden, either through some terminal illness or through being incapacitated, you wouldn't be able to get out of bed in the morning without somebody else doing it for you.  And basically doing everything for you.  Trapped inside nothing but your head, would it be worth it to you?</p>
<p>Or if you had a degenerative disease, knowing that being bedridden is the next step before the eventual release of death, how would you face each day?</p>
<p>Or if you went blind and/or deaf and while you <i>could</i> learn to live your life again, would you want to if many of life's pleasures were now denied you?</p>
<p>Or if you were locked up in prison for the rest of your life?  And would it make a difference if you were guilty or not guilty?</p>
<p>Or, if you were homeless, addicted to drugs, living on the street and disowned by all your family.  You don't have a bed to get out of, but how would you feel about facing the relentless stream of days (and nights)?</p>
<p>Or, if after being married for forty years, your spouse were to die, taking with them most of your human interaction for the day, support in all manner of practical activities, and half your memories?  Would you still feel it was worth carrying on?</p>
<p>In short, when every morning you wake up and your first thought is "oh, no, not again, how am I going to muster what it takes to get through this day?" can you honestly say you would bound out of bed to face it?</p>
<p>There are no right or wrong answers to these questions. Apart from anything else, there are so many details here that I cannot cover that almost all of us could be swayed from one side to the other depending on how you filled in the details.</p>
<p>And yet, society as a whole says that one answer is preferred over the other. To keep on going and to face these problems with valour is a noble thing to do.  At least, our society does.  Societies in the past, and I believe even some today, recognised life as a cycle in which knowing when your time was up and being ready to give up your life for the good of the tribe was the most noble of sacrifices.  For the good of the community.</p>
<p>All of us die sooner or later. Is there an inherent virtue in it being later? Why?</p>
<p>So if we're agreed that right now, "giving up" is not an option, let's do a though experiment: imagine on your alarm clock, in addition to "off" and "snooze", there's a "give up" button. Would you push it? What would you expect to happen?</p>
<p>There have definitely been times when I would have pushed it.  The interesting thing is that whenever I consider the reasons I would, or would not, push it, it always comes back to other people.  Other people with whom I have some sort of connection.  Most important, of course, are those closest to me - my immediate family.  Historically, they would all have suffered if I had not been here.  My wife still would.  But what if she had passed away before me?  What would be stopping me then?</p>
<p>From time to time as I contemplate this, I think about things that I have not done and want to - or things that I have promised people that I think I should deliver.  For me, "contracts" are huge.  If I "owe" somebody something, then it's important for me to stick at it and make it happen.  Even if that means carrying on living: so I can't push the "give up" button.</p>
<p>Some people argue that death is something that happens naturally, and therefore you shouldn't interfere with it. Or that, since you can't give life, it's unreasonable to take it away.</p>
<p>I don't accept that either of these arguments are even logical.  Rain is something that happens naturally, and yet I have both an umbrella to stop it falling on my head and a hose to water my garden.  Animals die naturally, and yet we have abbatoirs.  Trees die naturally, and yet we clear forests to build housing estates.</p>
<p>And in my life, I have given life twice: that is something I feel more guilty about.  I did not (could not) seek their consent before giving birth.  They (like me) are stuck here, having to make the best of it they can and the only people they can blame for their predicament are their parents.  Of course, if you love life, you probably don't see it that way and are eternally grateful to your parents for bringing you here.  Call me ungrateful, but, no, I'm not glad to have had that thrust on me.</p>
<p>So I come back to alternatives and choices.  In so much of life we are told that individual freedom is paramount.  But in this most individual of choices, apparently it's not.  It's not even acceptable to do what I am doing here and say it <i>is</i> a choice.  A very real choice, not just a limited one in which people <i>in extremis</i> can decide to curtail the pain by a few months, but one in which any rational human being can make a rational choice.</p>
<p>And what is that choice?  That choice is that, looking ahead for as far as they can see, there is not going to be - or likely to be - a point at which they are going to want to be jumping out of bed to face the day.  Yes, it might happen, but it is so unlikely that living through the years of purgatory to find out whether they were right or wrong <i>is just not worth it</i>.</p>
<p>And because I say it is a <i>rational</i> choice, it must be one you can - and should - express to those around you, specifically those most affected by your choice and have them engage with in a loving and rational way, not just offer a kneejerk reaction that you can't do that.  After all, you have plenty of time and life to think about it before deciding.  And it's also something where I feel most people should talk to an appropriate professional about their situation - again, one who is capable of having a rational discussion about this.</p>
<p>So, what do I think should happen if you push the "give up" button on your alarm clock?  It should notify those around you that you are struggling to carry on, and it should notify a professional who can facilitate those discussions.  All of those discussions should be rational, not emotional (although obviously everyone will bring very strong emotions to the table) and all the options should be on the table.</p>
<p>And anyone who says "no, I don't think you should give up" is putting on themselves a debt: a debt that they will follow through on whatever they agree to in order to convince that person to keep going.</p>
<p>Walking away from life may be rational, or it may be irrational.  Choosing to carry on may be rational or it may be irrational.  For me, every decision I make in life should be as rational as possible and having people just state something as a fact when it isn't makes me want to throttle them.</p>
<p>While I'm here, I'd like to make the case for changing the language about choosing death over life.  I'm happy for the phrases "committing suicide" and "killing yourself" to continue in use for the cases where (particularly young) people choose to end their life suddenly and "in the moment": tomorrow is just too hard to face, even if next week might be fine.  They feel they have a mountain to climb, but there is another side to come down.</p>
<p>But for the situations I'm describing, where life - at least a <i>good</i> life - is coming to an end, it feels there needs to be a softer phrase to describe acquiescing to that rather than insisting on fighting to the bitter end.  "Walking away" seems a good compromise, with all its connotations of walking away from everything else bad in life.  At the end of the day, death is not the enemy - a bad death is.  It is up to each of us to decide individually what constitutes a good death and a bad death for us.</p>
